diff --git a/docs/DOCUMENTATION.md b/docs/DOCUMENTATION.md
index 32d22b23fb..23009a2b3f 100644
--- a/docs/DOCUMENTATION.md
+++ b/docs/DOCUMENTATION.md
@@ -12568,5 +12568,62 @@ a strict, non-sandbox-specific bug fix that changes nothing about what data a re

 ---

+## Per-server SSH management key — Firewall Sync/SFTP/DB/website tools survive key-only guests (2026-09-26)
+
+**The incident.** A real customer (ticket `cmuifib3r0yvkhladt4m9ggtp`) disabled `PasswordAuthentication`
+on their VPS (running key-only SSH themselves, which is good practice). Firewall Sync started failing
+with ssh2's literal "All configured authentication methods failed" — because `withServerSsh`
+(`lib/ssh-exec.ts`) and `withSftp` (`lib/sftp.ts`) had exactly ONE way to reach a guest: the root
+password stored on the `Server` row. Every in-portal tool that touches a guest over SSH — Firewall Sync,
+the file manager (SFTP), the database browser, the website manager, one-click apps — shares this same
+path, so ALL of them silently break the moment a customer locks down password auth. There was no key to
+hand the customer either; none had ever been generated.
+
+**The fix — a per-server management keypair, not one shared platform key.** Mirrors the existing
+`Server.rootPasswordEnc` pattern exactly (AES-256-GCM, `lib/crypto-box.ts`, keyed off `AUTH_SECRET`) so a
+single leaked key only ever exposes the ONE server it belongs to — the same blast-radius profile the
+password already has today, instead of introducing one skeleton key that would open every customer VM
+at once.
+
+- `Server.sshManagementKeyEnc` (encrypted OpenSSH-format private key) / `Server.sshManagementPublicKey`
+  (plain authorized_keys line — public keys aren't secret) — new nullable columns, null on any server not
+  yet provisioned/backfilled with one.
+- `lib/ssh-management-key.ts`'s `generateManagementKeypair()` shells out to the system `ssh-keygen`
+  rather than hand-building the OpenSSH wire format in JS — a key that will hold root on a customer VM is
+  the wrong place to risk a subtle binary-encoding bug.
+- `worker/index.ts`'s `provision()` now generates one at provision time, alongside the customer's own
+  keys, and pushes the public half into cloud-init's `sshkeys` field the same way.
+- `withServerSsh` / `withSftp` try the stored key FIRST, falling back to the password only if there's no
+  key yet or the connect itself fails (e.g. a customer deleted our line from their own
+  `authorized_keys`) — a business-logic error from inside the callback never triggers a second, duplicate
+  attempt, only a genuine connect failure does.
+- `scripts/backfill-ssh-management-keys.ts` — one-off, idempotent, `--apply`-gated backfill for every
+  server provisioned before this shipped: connects with the CURRENT password (the only thing that still
+  works), pushes the key in, then stores it. A server already running key-only SSH can't be backfilled
+  this way (nothing left that can authenticate) — those need the customer to add the public key
+  themselves once support hands it to them, or briefly re-enable password auth for the one-time push.
+- **Dashboard**: `VpsCredentialsCard` (customer VPS "Root access" card) now has a "Management key"
+  section — shows the real public key text (safe to display in full) once installed, so a customer can
+  verify it in their own `authorized_keys` without filing a ticket; shows a plain "not installed yet"
+  note otherwise. This is literally what the customer asked support for in the first place.
+
+**Real, not just reasoned-about, verification.** Typecheck clean, full build passes, full suite run —
+same pre-existing failure set as the last audit (billing/promo/hazard-scan tests unrelated to this
+change; none of the 40 failures touch `lib/ssh-exec.ts`, `lib/sftp.ts`, `worker/index.ts`'s cloud-init
+path, or the new schema fields).
+
+**Not done yet, deliberately:** the backfill script has not been run against the live production fleet —
+it writes to every existing customer's `authorized_keys`, which is exactly the kind of blast-radius
+action that needs a human decision on timing, not something to fire unattended. Also deliberately out of
+scope for this pass: a self-service "rotate" or "remove" control on the dashboard — removing the key
+breaks the same tools it exists to protect, and rotating requires reaching the server, so both are real
+enough decisions to ship as their own deliberate follow-up rather than bundling into this build.
+
+**Files touched:** `prisma/schema.prisma`, `lib/ssh-management-key.ts` (new), `lib/sftp.ts`,
+`lib/ssh-exec.ts`, `worker/index.ts`, `scripts/backfill-ssh-management-keys.ts` (new),
+`components/customer/vpspanel/v2/VpsCredentialsCard.tsx`, `app/dashboard/servers/[id]/page.tsx`.
+
+---
+
 *Companion docs: `CLAUDE.md` (agent/ops conventions), `ROADMAP.md` (backlog), `docs/MIGRATION.md`
 (server migration).*
